	abstract = {Wildfires present a significant risk to the reliable operation of power systems, often causing power outages and disruptions, particularly in wildland-urban interface areas. This research addresses the challenge of balancing operational efficiency and social convenience during wildfire events. We propose a novel approach to enhancing power system resiliency through the coordination of three optimization methods: priori, posteriori, and interactive (PPI). A learning-based surrogate model is introduced to simulate wildfire effects on decision-maker preferences, such as priority and risk levels. The surrogate model operates in three phases: pre-training, training, and fine-tuning, to provide accurate and adaptive solutions. Computational tests on two case studies demonstrate the effectiveness of the model in mitigating wildfire impacts and ensuring resilient electricity supply. The results emphasize the potential of PPI optimization methods to enhance power system resiliency during extreme wildfire events. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
