	abstract = {Independent microgrids are increasingly deployed to serve critical loads in isolated or adversarial environments. However, their exposure to high-impact, low-probability disruptions necessitates accurate vulnerability assessment and topology optimization. Conventional approaches rely on Monte Carlo simulation (MCS) with time-series power flow analysis, requiring several minutes per scenario and making them incompatible with iterative optimization algorithms. Existing machine learning surrogates reduce this computational burden but often lack accuracy or explainability. To address these limitations, this study proposes a fast and explainable vulnerability assessment framework coupling MCS with a graph attention network enhanced by self-attention pooling (GAT-S). The framework integrates a comprehensive data generation workflow with power flow calculations and employs GAT-S for vulnerability prediction. GAT-S effectively integrates node- and graph-level information, achieving mean squared error below 0.001 across 33-, 66-, and 100-bus test systems, statistically outperforming existing state-of-the-art methods. The learned attention weights identify critical buses, providing both prediction explainability and enabling targeted hardening strategies. When deployed as a surrogate within evolutionary topology optimization, GAT-S reduces vulnerability assessment runtime from minutes to under one second while maintaining solution quality. Comprehensive experiments validate the framework’s accuracy, explainability, and generalization capabilities. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
