	abstract = {The growing shift toward renewable energy systems has intensified the demand for efficient energy storage devices, with supercapacitors emerging as key contenders due to their high power density, rapid charge–discharge capability, and long cycle life. However, improving their energy density and optimizing performance remain persistent challenges. Artificial intelligence (AI) offers a powerful route to accelerate material discovery and device optimization by predicting electrochemical performance from structural, morphological, and compositional features. This review provides a systematic analysis of AI models including artificial neural networks (ANN), random forest regression (RFR), support vector regression (SVR), gaussian process regression (GPR), linear and polynomial regression (LPR), and extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), applied to supercapacitor performance prediction across diverse electrode materials such as MXenes, carbon-based materials, metal oxides/dichalcogenides, and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs). Unlike existing reviews, this work integrates harmonized performance metrics, task-wise comparisons, and model interpretability insights (SHAP, permutation importance) while explicitly considering dataset size, leakage-safe validation, and fixed electrochemical testing conditions. The review identifies clear model-specific strengths such as ANN's nonlinear mapping capability, GPR's uncertainty quantification, and XGBoost's scalability, alongside persisting challenges related to data scarcity, standardization, and generalizability. By consolidating literature insights and benchmark comparisons, this review offers practical guidance for selecting suitable AI models and supports the development of AI-assisted approaches for designing next-generation supercapacitors in high-performance energy storage systems. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
