	doi = {10.1016/j.applthermaleng.2025.127556},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010848639?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Molten salt tanks play a pivotal role in solar thermal power generation, a technology increasingly adopted worldwide due to its potential to improve the efficiency and reliability of renewable energy storage. This study tackles the challenge of optimizing the structural design of molten salt tank wall panels, focusing on reducing wall mass while ensuring thermal and mechanical stability under varying operational conditions. The primary objective is to minimize wall mass while controlling thermal stresses and deformations that could otherwise undermine the system's overall performance. To achieve this, we employ a Multi-Objective Genetic Algorithm (MOGA) in conjunction with Response Surface Methodology (RSM) for multi-objective optimization. Latin Hypercube Sampling is utilized to generate experimental designs, with wall thickness and height as key design variables. The thermo-mechanical performance is evaluated based on three primary objectives: minimizing maximum thermal stress, reducing deformation, and minimizing wall mass. Our findings reveal that thermo-mechanical coupling effects cause wall stresses to increase as liquid levels rise, with the highest stress (125 MPa) observed at the weld zone. However, the critical stress targeted by the optimization occurs within the wall panels, where it peaks at 105 MPa in the first and second layers. The dome area experiences the most significant deformation (up to 0.16 m) due to minimal thermal constraints, while other regions of the wall require deformation flexibility to prevent additional stresses. The Pareto optimal solution derived from MOGA results in a 7 % reduction in wall mass, a 26.8 % reduction in maximum thermal stress, and a 0.6 % reduction in deformation compared to the original design under full-tank conditions. This study offers an efficient and lightweight design approach for molten salt tanks and contributes to global efforts to optimize renewable energy systems. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
