	abstract = {Electric tractors face persistent challenges in energy efficiency and battery degradation due to frequent and abrupt load fluctuations. Hence, this paper proposes an adaptive multi-objective energy management strategy (EMS) for E-tractors equipped with hybrid energy storage systems (HESS). A multi-source dynamics model is developed by integrating nonlinear tire–soil interaction, terrain slope variation, and resistance to characterize transient load behavior under diverse operating conditions. To improve system responsiveness, a scenario condition reconstruction (SCR) method is proposed, combining micro-trip segmentation with a deep residual neural network (DRNN), achieving 92.41% accuracy in online operating condition identification. Building on this, a cooperative EMS framework is proposed, integrating SCR with a Beluga Whale Optimization-enhanced radial basis function neural network (SCR with BWO-RBFNN). This lightweight metaheuristic controller enables adaptive power allocation across storage units under millisecond-level load disturbances, while a dynamic weighting mechanism coordinates trade-offs between energy efficiency and battery lifespan. The results indicate that, compared with the traditional battery-only power system, the proposed HESS powertrain increases the final battery state of charge (SoC) by 18.9% and reduces the total cost of ownership (TCO) by 26.9%. Furthermore, under the same HESS configuration, the proposed SCR with BWO-RBFNN improves the final battery SoC by 11.8% and reduces the TCO by approximately 21.6% compared with the deterministic rule-based EMS. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed strategy in enhancing energy utilization and reducing lifecycle costs under complex operating conditions. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
