	abstract = {A hybrid renewable energy system capable of simultaneously generating electricity, freshwater, cooling, and hydrogen was proposed. The system harnessed geothermal and solar energy through a concentrating solar power (CSP)-assisted geothermal flash cycle, coupled with a combined power and multi-cooling cycle (CPMCC) that used carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) as the working fluid. The CPMCC integrated a supercritical CO<sub>2</sub> power cycle for efficient energy conversion and a dual-stage ejector refrigeration system to provide cooling at two distinct temperature levels. The configuration included a proton exchange membrane electrolyzer for hydrogen generation, and two desalination technologies— humidification–dehumidification (HDH) and multi-effect desalination (MED)—for freshwater production. A detailed exergy and thermodynamic analysis was conducted to evaluate system performance and identify major sources of inefficiency. The results indicated that the desalination subsystems and the CPMCC steam generator contributed most significantly to overall exergy destruction. A machine learning–assisted, three-objective optimization framework was employed to investigate two design scenarios: one prioritizing electricity generation and the other freshwater production. The findings demonstrated that output priorities could be shifted with only minor reductions in overall exergy efficiency. The integration of surrogate modeling with genetic algorithms enabled rapid and accurate optimization. The optimized scenarios yielded a substantial improvement in net present value (NPV) relative to the base case. A case study for Madinah, Saudi Arabia confirmed the seasonal reliability and techno-economic feasibility of the system under real-world solar and geothermal conditions. © 2025 The Institution of Chemical Engineers},
