	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105021232924?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Thermal management in high-power electronics and energy systems demands advanced cooling solutions. The ultra-maximum hydraulic diameter pulsating heat pipe (UHDPHP) offers superior heat dissipation but lacks systematic parameter studies, particularly regarding the evaporator-to-condenser length ratio. This work investigates the start-up behavior and thermal performance of an UHDPHP using single-factor experiments and response surface methodology (RSM). The effects of length ratio (1–2.5), filling ratio (30–60 %), heat load (40–400 W), and inclination angle (30–90°) were evaluated. Results indicate that a 50 % filling ratio enables optimal start-up and minimal thermal resistance. Thermal resistance exhibits a non-monotonic dependence on length ratio, reaching a minimum at a length ratio of 2 across all heat loads. Although a length ratio of 1 ensures the fastest start-up, a ratio of 1.5 yields better temperature uniformity with a mean deviation of 3.44 ℃. RSM optimization revealed strong interactions between length ratio and filling ratio, identifying an optimal region (filling ratio: 45–55 %, length ratio: 1.6–2.3, heat load > 340 W) where thermal resistance falls below 0.02 ℃/W). The quadratic model predicted a global minimum thermal resistance of 0.0144 ℃/W at heat load of 390.5 W, filling ratio of 46.1 %, and length ratio of 1.87, outperforming all tested conditions. This work presents the first comprehensive analysis and optimization of length ratio effects in UHDPHPs, offering practical guidelines for designing large-diameter PHPs. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.},
