	abstract = {Recently, successive ultra-high voltage transformer turret explosions in China have caused significant economic losses, posing a substantial threat to power system safety. This paper presents a method for preventing turret explosions under high-energy arcing faults using aluminium foam sandwich panels, and the global optimal solution for mitigating the impact of arcing faults by incorporating graded densities of aluminium foam is determined by the surrogate model. First, to analyze the failure behaviour of the turret and the material response for the aluminium foam sandwich panels, a bubble–fluid–structure coupling numerical approach is proposed. On this basis, the energy absorption and maximum displacement of the sandwich panel are predicted using a radial basis function (RBF) surrogate model. With the constructed surrogate model, the multiobjective particle swarm optimization (MOPSO) approach is utilized for obtaining the global optimal values of the design variables, including face sheet thickness and core layer density. The simulation results show that the sandwich panel can effectively prevent turret explosions under a high-energy arcing fault of 5 MJ. Furthermore, compared to uniform-density aluminium foam of equal mass, the optimized design achieves an 11.6 % reduction in maximum stress and a 20.4 % reduction in the maximum displacement of the turret. © 2025 The Institution of Chemical Engineers},
