	abstract = {This paper addresses the challenge of achieving optimal and secure operation in coal mine integrated energy systems (CMIES) under renewable energy generation (REG) uncertainty and carbon reduction constraints. Existing approaches often overlook the deep coupling between coal production and energy flows or rely on overly conservative uncertainty handling. To address these gaps, a novel coal-energy-carbon-coupled CMIES model is developed. This model integrates coal production, energy supply, and carbon emission control modules, aiming for the synergistic minimization of operational costs and carbon emissions while ensuring coal production energy demand is met. Furthermore, a data-driven flexible adjustable robust optimization (DFARO) framework is proposed to manage REG fluctuations. By utilizing neural network surrogate model to establish a data-driven confidence level for the uncertainty set and integrating information gap decision theory (IGDT) with adjustable robust optimization (ARO), the framework ensures the system can achieve predefined operational objectives by balancing flexibility and robustness, even under worst-case uncertainty scenarios, thus guaranteeing the secure and economic operation of CMIES. Then, a bi-level iterative solution method, combining the stochastic dichotomy method and the column-and-constraint generation (C&CG) algorithm, is designed for efficient computation. Finally, a case study based on a real-world coal mine in China demonstrates that the proposed CMIES model reduces operational costs by 4.64% and carbon emissions by 7.66%. Notably, the DFARO framework ensures a 100% feasibility ratio under uncertainty, significantly outperforming the 72.36% achieved by deterministic model. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
