	doi = {10.1109/UPEC65436.2025.11279933},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031355574?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper investigates the impact of distributed energy resource (DER) penetration levels on the feasibility of AI-based surrogate models for solving the optimal power flow (OPF) problem. Using a realistic distribution network with photovoltaic and wind-based DERs, a surrogate model is trained on OPF solutions optimized for loss minimization and evaluated across a range of penetration levels, from 5% to 60% of peak load. The results reveal that while the surrogate achieves high feasibility (>96%) at low penetration levels (5-30%), performance deteriorates at higher levels, reaching approximately 88% at 60% penetration. Constraint violations are strongly tied to DER characteristics: voltage magnitude violations occur at buses hosting large-capacity DERs, while apparent power violations are concentrated at small DER units with tight inverter margins. These findings highlight the sensitivity of OPF surrogate models to DER-induced operational constraints and underscore the challenges of deploying them reliably in high-penetration distribution networks.  © 2025 IEEE.},
