	abstract = {Smart energy hub (EH) networks are being developed as a promising solution for the next generation of electricity and gas distribution. However, the telecommunication technologies used in smart grids are vulnerable to malicious attack posing a constant threat to demand-supply structure. Cyber threats on these infrastructures have had a significant impact on various systems, including electrical and thermal storage (TS) units. Given the critical importance of cybersecurity in these networks, machine learning (ML) is expected to play a significant role in enhancing security. These algorithms are capable of identifying anomalies and deviations from normal behavior, which are often signs of malicious activities. The primary aim of this study is to use intelligent learning algorithms to detection malicious attacks on the demand side. The impact of malicious attack on the demand for multiple energy carriers is examined, with particular attention to the demand-supply structure of the EH system. Data from different scenarios are then applied in detective algorithms. This study focuses on optimizing the operational cost of an EH through the use of the CPLEX solver and mixed integer linear programming (MILP) model. Furthermore, the effects of false data injection (FDI) on actual network loads, which can disrupt energy management, have also been investigated. Different attack scenarios that target both electric and thermal loads are being analyzed, considering whether attackers have prior knowledge of the EH's demand-supply structure. The results highlight that attacks with knowledge of the demand-supply structure have a significantly larger impact, leading to changes in electric load during peak hours that are up to 17.4 % higher compared to attacks without prior knowledge. The findings indicate that the support vector machine (SVM) with a radial basis function (RBF) has achieved the highest accuracy at 91.67 %, followed by the SVM with a quadratic polynomial function (QPF) at 88.89 %. Additionally, the three-layer artificial neural network (ANN) algorithm has reached an accuracy of 83.33 %, while logistic regression (LR) has achieved 77.78 %. Although malicious cyber-attack can compromise the effectiveness of the data recognition data detection, the utilize of intelligent learning methods can still provide a strong defense against such failures. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
