	abstract = {The security-constrained unit commitment (SCUC) problem is characterized by its high-dimensional, non-convex, and mixed-integer nonlinear nature. The incorporation of grid security constraints significantly expands the model scale, resulting in low computational efficiency. To improve solving performance, this paper proposes a data-driven solution method for SCUC. First, a surrogate model for AC power flow calculation is constructed using a CNN-BLS (Convolutional Neural Network-Broad Learning System) architecture. By training on extensive historical power flow data, a nonlinear mapping between system operating states and power flow results is established. Furthermore, the implicit relationship between generator output and line power flow is derived. Using sensitivity analysis, the complex AC power flow constraints are transformed into linear constraints expressed in terms of generation power variables. Consequently, the SCUC problem is reformulated into a mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) model, which is solved using Cplex. Simulation results on the 10 -unit 39-bus system demonstrate that the proposed method significantly reduces solution time while maintaining accuracy, thereby effectively enhancing the computational efficiency of the SCUC problem.  © 2025 IEEE.},
