	abstract = {In traditional security-constrained dispatch, the evaluation of the transmission network's post-contingency state frequently relies on static, pre-computed power transfer distribution factors (PTDFs). This approach, which must be repeated for every new N-1 topology, is no longer able to meet the real-time decision-making requirements of modern power systems. Hence, this paper suggests a precise surrogate modeling framework for evaluating post-contingency states, employing a Graph Neural Network (GNN). Firstly, a GNN is trained offline on a large dataset of simulated N-1 contingencies to learn the complex mapping from grid topology and nodal injections to post-contingency line flows. Next, the GNN is utilized online to provide an instantaneous prediction of the base line flows for a given contingency. Furthermore, by applying numerical perturbation to the GNN's inputs, a data-driven sensitivity matrix (analogous to PTDF) is derived. By utilizing the theory of linear programming (LP), the problem of solving the corrective dispatch is transformed into determining an optimal set-point based on the GNN-predicted base flow and the GNN-derived sensitivity matrix. This approach achieves the replacement of the computationally expensive, repeated physics-based model-building (DC solve and PTDF recomputation) with fast neural network inference. The efficacy of the suggested approach is showcased by evaluating and analyzing the IEEE 118-bus test system.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {corrective control; DC power flow; Energy storage dispatch; graph neural network; linear programming; N-1 security},
