	abstract = {This paper proposes a deep deterministic policy gradient (DDPG)-based framework for energy-efficient edge intelligence in mobile edge computing (MEC) power grid networks, with a particular focus on task dependency. The proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) devices and the increasing complexity of applications have resulted in a massive surge in data generation at the network edge. While MEC networks provide low-latency and high-bandwidth processing capabilities, their limited energy resources pose a significant challenge to scalability and sustainability. Moreover, the dependencies among tasks executed within these networks further complicate resource management and scheduling. To address these challenges, we propose a DDPG-based approach that leverages reinforcement learning to optimize the task scheduling and resource allocation in MEC power grid networks, with a particular emphasis on task dependency. By modeling the MEC network as a Markov decision process (MDP) and incorporating task dependencies into the state representation, our framework enables the DDPG agent to learn an optimal policy that minimizes the energy consumption while satisfying application-specific quality of service (QoS) requirements. Through extensive simulations, we evaluate the performance of the proposed framework and demonstrate its superiority over benchmark algorithms. Our results show that by considering task dependency, the DDPG-based approach can achieve significant energy savings while improving the QoS metrics such as task completion time and task success rate. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2024.},
