	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105011060452?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Low-Voltage DC microgrids that incorporate batteries and supercapacitors are effective in managing energy fluctuations. This document outlines a voltage regulation controller developed for a photovoltaic DC microgrid that incorporates a hybrid energy storage system (HESS). The controller incorporates an inner Proportional-Integral (PI) current loop alongside an outer Neural Network-Based Fractional Order Proportional-Integral (NNFOPI) voltage loop. The NNFOPI controller sets the overall current reference, which is subsequently distributed to storage devices through a low-pass filter. The fractional order PI (FOPI) controller is designed using small-signal modeling of the bi-directional DC-DC converter (BDC). The parameters are optimized utilizing an Artificial Neural Network (ANN). The artificial neural network (ANN) is designed with three input parameters and two output results, serving as a surrogate model to enhance the efficiency of optimization procedures. The simulations performed with MATLAB/SIMULINK confirm the effectiveness of the proposed controller when compared to a cascaded PI controller. The results indicate superior disturbance resistance and an enhanced dynamic response, as evidenced by metrics related to undershoot and settling time. © 2025 IEEE.},
