	abstract = {The design of aircraft thermal management and power systems requires the determination of the architecture, component design, and control strategies necessary to ensure the successful completion of anticipated flight missions. The performance of these subsystems and their underlying components is typically dependent upon multiple parameters, whose values remain fixed throughout the duration of the flight. To gain insight into the dominant parameters driving the response of these subsystems, and thereby more intelligently conduct their design, sensitivity analysis may be performed. Global sensitivity analysis methods, in particular, may be used to quantify the influence of candidate parameters upon outputs of interest throughout the entire range of the multi-dimensional parameter space. The current paper illustrates the application of Sobol’s variance-based approach to two analysis cases i) coolant supply temperature set point deviation sensitivity in a vapor cycle thermal management system subject to a pulsed thermal load ii) simplifying multi-objective optimization based design of a salient pole, wound field synchronous generator. The APTT (Aircraft Power / Thermal Toolkit) Sensitivity Analysis Tool developed at the Air Force Research Laboratory is used to pre-process, execute, and post-process results for the aforementioned global sensitivity analysis by providing an interface between mathematical models developed in either MATLAB or SIMULINK to sensitivity analysis methods in DAKOTA. Gaussian Process surrogate models are also created by DAKOTA to accelerate the computation of sensitivity indices. Results indicate that non-negligible interactions are present between input factors influencing the supply set point temperature deviation, while an approximately additive model may be inferred for the input factors investigated to simplify the multi-objective optimization-based generator design. Histograms and 95% confidence intervals for skewness were also